    "result_text": "All 55 tests pass with a clean build. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a structured address form to the `POST /api/owners` endpoint while keeping the flat `address` input backward-compatible.\n\n**OpenAPI spec** (`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`) \u2014 added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` string properties to `OwnerFields`, and removed `address` from the `required` list so the generated DTO no longer forces `@NotNull` on it (an owner can now supply the structured form instead).\n\n**Owner model** (`Owner.java`) \u2014 added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` fields (columns `address_line1`/`address_line2`) with getters/setters so MapStruct persists and echoes them.\n\n**DB schema** \u2014 added the two columns to all four dialect schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n**Controller** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeAddress`) \u2014 now normalizes `addressLine1`, `addressLine2`, and the flat `address` with the same rules (trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, expand street abbreviations), then composes the flat `address`:\n- When a non-blank `addressLine1` is present, `address` = normalized `addressLine1`, with a single space + normalized `addressLine2` appended when `addressLine2` is present (structured preferred).\n- Otherwise it keeps the normalized flat value.\n\nBecause the composed `address` is set before validation, the existing required-field check naturally enforces \"an address in either form, plus city\" (composed value is blank only when neither form was supplied). Everything reading the address downstream \u2014 the household hash, postcode validation, and locality \u2014 already keys off `postcode`/`city` (structured fields), so those correctly reflect the structured input.\n\nThe MapStruct mapper auto-mapped the new fields both ways (verified in the generated impl), so `addressLine1`, `addressLine2`, and the composed `address` are all returned on read.",
